<section class="intro" id="about">

<div class="section-heading">

<span class="small">UNDERSTANDING THE ISSUE</span>

<h2>
What Are Pharmaceutical Residues?
</h2>

<p>
Pharmaceutical residues are medicines, their metabolites,
or transformation products that can remain in wastewater
and the environment after medicines are used or released.
</p>

</div>


<div class="intro-grid">

<div class="info-box">

<h3>
An Invisible Pollutant
</h3>

<p>
Unlike plastic waste or oil spills, pharmaceutical
pollution may not be visible to the human eye.
Compounds such as antibiotics, painkillers, hormones
and other medicines can occur in water at very low
concentrations.
</p>

<br>

<p>
The challenge is that many pharmaceutical substances
are designed to produce biological effects. Even when
present in small amounts, researchers investigate how
continuous environmental exposure may affect aquatic
organisms.
</p>

</div>


<div class="highlight">

<h3>
Why is this becoming important?
</h3>

<p>
Modern medicine has transformed human health.
At the same time, increasing pharmaceutical use,
population growth and improved analytical techniques
have increased attention to pharmaceutical residues
in water.
</p>

<br>

<p>
The issue connects healthcare, wastewater management,
environmental science and responsible medicine disposal.
</p>

</div>

</div>

</section>


