<section class="sources" id="sources">

<div class="section-heading">

<span class="small">WHERE DOES IT COME FROM?</span>

<h2>
Major Sources of Pharmaceutical Pollution
</h2>

<p>
Medicines can enter water systems through several
different pathways.
</p>

</div>


<div class="source-grid">


<div class="source-card">

<div class="source-icon">💊</div>

<h3>Human Excretion</h3>

<p>
After taking medicine, the body may excrete some active
ingredients or metabolites through urine and feces.
These substances can enter sewage systems.
</p>

</div>


<div class="source-card">

<div class="source-icon">🗑️</div>

<h3>Improper Disposal</h3>

<p>
Throwing medicines into sinks, toilets or household waste
can allow pharmaceutical compounds to enter wastewater
or the environment.
</p>

</div>


<div class="source-card">

<div class="source-icon">🏥</div>

<h3>Hospitals</h3>

<p>
Hospitals and healthcare facilities use many medicines,
creating pharmaceutical waste that requires appropriate
management.
</p>

</div>


<div class="source-card">

<div class="source-icon">🏭</div>

<h3>Manufacturing</h3>

<p>
Pharmaceutical manufacturing can generate wastewater
containing active pharmaceutical ingredients when
environmental controls are inadequate.
</p>

</div>


<div class="source-card">

<div class="source-icon">🐄</div>

<h3>Agriculture & Livestock</h3>

<p>
Veterinary medicines can enter soil and water through
animal waste and agricultural runoff.
</p>

</div>


<div class="source-card">

<div class="source-icon">🌧️</div>

<h3>Runoff</h3>

<p>
Rainfall can transport pharmaceutical contaminants from
land surfaces into nearby waterways.
</p>

</div>

</div>

</section>


<!-- =====================================================
     JOURNEY
===================================================== -->

<section class="journey" id="journey">

<div class="section-heading">

<span class="small">FOLLOW THE PATH</span>

<h2>
How Does a Medicine Reach a River?
</h2>

<p>
A simple example of how pharmaceutical residues can move
through the water cycle.
</p>

</div>


<div class="journey-line">


<div class="step">

<div class="step-dot"></div>

<div class="step-content">

<h3>1. Medicine is taken</h3>

<p>
A person uses a medicine according to medical advice.
Some of the substance may be metabolized by the body.
</p>

</div>

</div>


<div class="step">

<div class="step-dot"></div>

<div class="step-content">

<h3>2. Residues enter sewage</h3>

<p>
Active ingredients or metabolites may leave the body
through urine or feces and enter wastewater.
</p>

</div>

</div>


<div class="step">

<div class="step-dot"></div>

<div class="step-content">

<h3>3. Wastewater treatment</h3>

<p>
Treatment plants remove many pollutants. However,
removal efficiency differs between pharmaceutical
compounds and treatment processes.
</p>

</div>

</div>


<div class="step">

<div class="step-dot"></div>

<div class="step-content">

<h3>4. Treated water is released</h3>

<p>
After treatment, water may be discharged into rivers,
lakes, coastal waters or other receiving environments.
</p>

